WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.997
This exercise asks you to complete an INSERT for a new patient.

00:00:03.997 --> 00:00:06.867
Most of the statement is already provided.

00:00:06.867 --> 00:00:12.202
Your job is to supply a new unique medical record number in the missing location.

00:00:12.202 --> 00:00:14.602
Think first about the constraint.

00:00:14.602 --> 00:00:21.737
Medical record number is unique, so reusing an existing value should cause PostgreSQL to reject the insert.

00:00:21.737 --> 00:00:27.702
Enter a new quoted value, run the statement, and inspect what RETURNING shows.

00:00:27.702 --> 00:00:32.592
The exercise is not about memorizing the entire INSERT syntax.

00:00:32.592 --> 00:00:37.532
Focus on the relationship between the target column list and the values list.

00:00:37.532 --> 00:00:40.532
If an insert fails, read the error.

00:00:40.532 --> 00:00:46.442
A constraint error can be useful because it tells you the database is protecting a rule defined in

00:00:46.442 --> 00:00:51.392
the schema. When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:51.392 --> 00:00:57.677
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:57.677 --> 00:01:00.147
and the shape of the expected output.

00:01:00.147 --> 00:01:05.667
In a real setting such as an application safely inserting or updating business records,

00:01:05.667 --> 00:01:11.827
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:01:11.827 --> 00:01:14.082
before discussing syntax.

00:01:14.082 --> 00:01:20.466
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
